  "text": "Question fourteen. What is an RNN, and why does it struggle with long sequences? A recurrent neural network reads a sequence one step at a time, updating one hidden state with the same weights each step. So the gradient from step fifty back to step one passes through fifty similar factors. If each halves it, one part in a thousand trillion is left: long-range patterns are forgotten, or, with factors above one, they explode. And step fifty can't start until step forty-nine is done, so a GPU can't work on a sequence's steps in parallel. Question fifteen. How do LSTMs and GRUs address that? The LSTM, or long short-term memory, adds a cell state changed mostly by addition, under input, forget and output gates. Along it, the gradient meets only the forget gate: at point nine five, fifty steps keep about eight percent. The GRU, gated recurrent unit, uses two gates and no separate cell: three blocks of weights to the LSTM's four, for similar results. But both still read one step at a time. Attention did it better: every position reaches every other in one step, all at once, and language models are built on it. Next: NLP and large language models.",
